<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuño de Guzmán</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1490–<a href="/years/1558">1558</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nuño Beltrán de Guzmán was a Spanish conquistador and colonial official in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. As governor of Pánuco he raided the Huastec region for slaves, and as president of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> he clashed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>. In 1530 he burned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> ruler and led a brutal campaign into western Mexico, founding the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-galicia">New Galicia</a>. He was arrested in 1537.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuno-de-guzman.jpg" alt="Nuño de Guzmán" width="320" height="674" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NunoBeltranGuzman-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalajara-mexico">Guadalajara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, to an old noble family; his father, Hernán Beltrán de Guzmán, was a wealthy merchant and a high constable of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a>. The family backed Prince Charles during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-comuneros">Revolt of the Comuneros</a>. Guzmán studied law without taking a degree and served as one of the 100 royal bodyguards of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, going with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> in 1522. The crown made him governor of Pánuco in 1525 as a counterweight to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>, and he took up the post in May 1527.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Pánuco he stripped Cortés's supporters of property and had some tried and executed, and he organised a trade in Indian slaves: on a raid along the Río de Las Palmas in 1528 every horseman could take 20 slaves, and in eight months he issued more than 1,500 slaving licences. The slaves were branded on the face and shipped to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. As president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> he banned direct letters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, so that Bishop Juan de Zumárraga hid one sealed in wax inside a cask, and on 7 March 1530 Zumárraga placed the Audiencia under interdict.</p><p>On 21 December 1529 Guzmán set out west with 300 to 400 conquistadors and 5,000 to 8,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> allies. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a> the cazonci Tangáxuan II received him peaceably with gifts of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, but was tortured for hidden treasure, dragged by a horse and burned alive on 14 February 1530. The campaign, which has been called a genocidal enterprise, razed villages and tortured their leaders through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayarit">Nayarit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinaloa">Sinaloa</a>, where he founded San Miguel de Culiacán on 29 September 1531. His captain Cristóbal de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onati">Oñate</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalajara-mexico">Guadalajara</a>, and his raids were a main cause of the Mixtón rebellion. He lost Pánuco in 1533 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-galicia">New Galicia</a> in 1534.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After an inquiry by Diego Pérez de la Torre he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in fetters and released from the castle of Torrejón in 1538. In a memorial of 1552 he called the cazonci's execution the most just carried out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. His will of 1558, found in 1973, shows him poor and in debt, leaving most of his belongings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a> de Guzmán, who had nursed him. He probably died in Valladolid on or soon after 16 October 1558. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernal-diaz-del-castillo">Bernal Díaz del Castillo</a> called him the most foul and evil man in New Spain; his biographer Donald Chipman calls him the personification of the Black Legend, a reputation shaped largely by his opponents, Cortés, Zumárraga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-de-quiroga">Vasco de Quiroga</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nuño de Guzmán” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Conquistadors spread out to subdue Oaxaca, Michoacán, Guatemala and the Gulf coast, while <b>Nuño de Guzmán</b>’s brutal campaigns in the 1530s created the Kingdom of New Galicia in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1529–1530 <b>Nuño de Guzmán</b>, president of the First Audiencia of New Spain, on his way to conquer western Mexico, arrested the king with the help of the Purépecha noble Pedro Cuiniarángari, tortured him in search of gold and tried him for rebellion, withholding tribute and other charges.</blockquote><a href="/empire/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Hernán Cortés subdued the region in 1522, and slave raiding under <b>Nuño de Guzmán</b> devastated it.</blockquote><a href="/state/huastec-chiefdoms">Huastec chiefdoms</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nuño de Guzmán</b> led a notoriously violent campaign through western Mexico in 1529-31, founding towns and naming the territory Nueva Galicia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-new-galicia">Kingdom of New Galicia</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Nuño de Guzmán</b> conquered western Mexico in 1529-31, the Cora (Náayeri) of the rugged Sierra del Nayar remained beyond Spanish control.</blockquote><a href="/state/nayar">Nayar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalajara-spain">Guadalajara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Torrejón de Velasco, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.63,-3.17&amp;year=1534" aria-label="Guadalajara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4472 712 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nuño de Guzmán"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4922 982h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4922 982h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.63,-3.17&amp;year=1534">What was here in 1534? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
